   "title": "A second, unauthorised DHCP server is handing out addresses",
   "summary": "192.168.1.66 answered DHCP requests and told 4 devices to use it as their router and DNS server.",
   "explanation": "When a device joins the network it asks \"who can give me an address?\" (DHCP). Your real router, 192.168.1.1, answers. But a second device, 192.168.1.66, raced it and won for 4 devices, telling them to send all their traffic and all their name lookups through it.",
   "analogy": "It is like someone standing at the office front door handing out fake maps, so new visitors walk through the stranger's room before reaching any meeting.",
   "cause": "A device on the network is running its own DHCP server. Usually this is an attacker doing a man-in-the-middle, sometimes a misplugged home router.",
   "fix": [
    "Find the device with MAC `02:66:de:ad:be:ef` (switch port or Wi-Fi client list) and disconnect it.",
    "Enable DHCP snooping on the switch so only the router's port may answer DHCP.",
    "On affected devices, renew the lease: `ipconfig /renew` (Windows) or `sudo dhclient -r && sudo dhclient` (Linux).",
    "Change any passwords typed on affected devices while this was happening."
   ],
   "technical": "DHCPOFFER/ACK from server-id 192.168.1.66 (02:66:de:ad:be:ef) with option 3 router=192.168.1.66 and option 6 dns=192.168.1.66, while the established server 192.168.1.1 offered router=192.168.1.1 dns=192.168.1.1. The rogue answered 4 of 5 DISCOVERs first (median 1.8 ms vs 14.2 ms).",
